Added characterization
- Reused the Lot-1 twin-target scenario-and-phase oracle with native Target-A active and Target-B explicitly pending.
- Confirmed boot backlog produces zero wake and one fresh selected envelope produces one exact, cryptographically verified signed wake in-process.
- Observed the exact signed instruction at real local-tmux and headless local-native PTYs.
- Confirmed local-native host runs under setsid with closed stdin, no controlling terminal, and an explicit PTY master/slave pair; the observer is in raw mode.
- Exercised escape-sequence chunks, split UTF-8 observation, partial PTY writes, TIOCSWINSZ, native-terminal frame overflow, oversized signed input, and pipe closure.
- Compared alive, wakeable, unavailable, stale, and key-revoked native state observations.
- Applied F1/F2/F3 with worker restart and concurrent retry.
- Independently observed post-effect/lost-receipt terminal wakes on local-tmux and local-native and proved the phase oracle rejects them as unreconciled.
- Proved a restart after a lost F2 receipt emits no second wake because the restarted handler seeds the envelope as boot backlog.
- Proved concurrent F2/F3 retries remain unsafe: both attempts create distinct signed nonces and can each emit a wake.
- Proved native wake-request dry-run leaves target and durable state unchanged for both host-kind scenarios.

Native gaps retained as explicit seams
- Target-B needs the enlarged cluster-mesh 0.9.0 contract/capability bundle.
- Native request identity has no target-registration, holder, or epoch binding and no durable receipt.
- F1 restart suppresses a known pre-effect retry because pending work becomes boot backlog.
- Concurrent F2/F3 retry can duplicate a wake; there is no durable reconciliation and the oracle refuses a green result.
- The current self-wake handler does not consume registration revocation or custody evidence; a revoked signer key does not stop physical wake dispatch.
- Contract-grade BUFFER_OVERFLOW, PIPE_CLOSED, and NO_CONTROLLING_TERMINAL outcomes and direct read-only mode remain unavailable.

